oteRef` modulo refspec direction\n**Severity:** Low\n**Category:** reuse\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/strategy/generation_repair.go:163-180` vs. `cmd/entire/cli/strategy/cleanup.go:582-600`\n**Issue:** Both helpers build the same `--force-with-lease=<ref>:<oid>` arg, call `remote.PushWithOptions`, and trim/wrap `result.Output` identically. Only the refspec differs: `\":\" + refName` (delete) vs. `sourceRef + \":\" + remoteRef` (push).\n**Impact if not fixed:** Two near-identical 18-line force-with-lease wrappers in the same package; any future change (e.g., adding `--atomic` or per-error retry) must be made twice.\n**Trade-offs:** A single `pushWithLease(ctx, target, refspec, leaseRef, expectedOID, errCtx string)` is straightforward; the only cost is a small API surface in `cleanup.go` or a new file for the helper.\n**Evidence:** Diff shown above. Both live in `strategy/`, both use `remote.PushOptions`.\n**Suggested fix:** Extract one helper (e.g., in `cleanup.go`) and have both call sites pass refspec + lease tuple; delete `pushRepairedV2Generation`.\n\n### Finding 3: `entire clean --all` now reads every transcript per archived generation\n**Severity:** Medium\n**Category:** efficiency / hot-path bloat\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/strategy/cleanup.go:373`\n**Issue:** Switched from `ComputeGenerationCheckpointTimestamps` (prefers cheap `/main` metadata.json lookups, falls back to top-of-transcript timestamp) to `ComputeGenerationRawTranscriptTimestamps` (always reads each checkpoint's full-tree). `ReadGeneration` (the truly cheap path) is only used as a *fallback* when raw is missing.\n**Impact if not fixed:** Cleanup runs on every `entire clean --all`, every interactive `entire clean`, and during cleanup eligibility evaluation. For repos with many archived generations × many checkpoints, this reads every transcript per invocation, even when the cached `generation.json` already says the answer. On large repos this is the difference between sub-second and many-seconds.\n**Trade-offs:** Reverting to `ComputeGenerationCheckpointTimestamps` here keeps cleanup fast. The migration repair path is the only consumer that genuinely needs the \"ignore stored generation.json, recompute from raw\" semantics — that is repair's job, not cleanup's. After repair runs at end of migration, archived `generation.json` is authoritative, so cleanup should prefer it.\n**Evidence:** Cleanup callsite at `cleanup.go:373`, fallback to `ReadGeneration` at `:380` is unreachable on healthy archives. The repair (`generation_repair.go:97`) is the only place that needs raw recomputation.\n**Suggested fix:** Revert cleanup to call `ComputeGenerationCheckpointTimestamps` first, then `ReadGeneration`. Keep the new raw walker scoped to repair only. (Or: have cleanup call `ReadGeneration` first since the repair pass already wrote authoritative metadata.)\n\n### Finding 4: `RepairV2GenerationMetadata` runs unconditionally on every migration invocation\n**Severity:** Medium\n**Category:** efficiency\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:95-99`\n**Issue:** After every `entire migrate` (including idempotent re-runs over already-migrated repos), repair iterates every archived generation and reads every checkpoint's raw transcript via `ComputeGenerationRawTranscriptTimestamps`, then compares against `ReadGeneration`. On repos with already-correct generation.json files the entire pass is wasted I/O.\n**Impact if not fixed:** Repeated migrate runs (CI, support workflows, partial recovery) re-walk all archived generation transcripts despite no change. Also: every archived ref is fetched from origin if remote-only (see `listArchivedV2GenerationCandidates`).\n**Trade-offs:** Skipping repair when the existing `generation.json` matches the raw recomputation costs one cheap `ReadGeneration` per archived ref; the current code does that *after* the expensive raw walk. Inverting the order (read stored generation.json; recompute only when needed for verification or when stored is missing) makes idempotent re-runs cheap.\n**Evidence:** `repairOneV2GenerationMetadata` (`generation_repair.go:80-97`) calls `ComputeGenerationRawTranscriptTimestamps` first, then `ReadGeneration`, then compares. The cheap path is reachable but always runs *after* the expensive one.\n**Suggested fix:** Read `generation.json` first; if it is present and well-formed (oldest/newest non-zero, oldest <= newest), skip raw recomputation. Only walk transcripts when stored metadata is missing or invalid. Alternatively, gate the entire repair pass behind `result.migrated > 0` so a no-op migrate doesn't trigger repair.\n\n### Finding 5: Peak heap holds every migrated transcript before packing\n**Severity:** Medium\n**Category:** efficiency\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:184, 211-222, 380-394`\n**Issue:** `migratedFullCheckpoints []migratedFullCheckpoint` accumulates `*checkpoint.SessionContent` (which holds the full transcript bytes) for every successfully-migrated checkpoint and every backfilled \"missing full\" checkpoint. Batching only happens at the end via slice slicing in `packMigratedFullGenerations`. Peak memory ~ sum of all migrated transcripts.\n**Impact if not fixed:** On a repo with hundreds of historical checkpoints with multi-MB transcripts, peak migration heap can be hundreds of MB, all retained until the final pack step. For large transcripts loaded from v1 (compact + raw) this risks OOM on small CI runners.\n**Trade-offs:** Streaming each batch (call `writeMigratedFullGeneration` whenever the in-memory list reaches `migrateMaxCheckpointsPerGeneration`) caps peak heap at one batch worth. The cost is interleaving git ref writes with checkpoint migration loop, making error handling slightly trickier — but `pack` already commits per-batch refs, so partial-progress semantics are unchanged.\n**Evidence:** `migratedFullCheckpoints = append(...)` at line 212 and 211; partition only at `packMigratedFullGenerations` (line 380+). `migratedFullSession.con