ilures leave local ahead of remote\n\n**Severity:** Low\n**Category:** correctness\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/strategy/generation_repair.go:117-136`\n**Issue:** The function calls `repo.Storer.SetReference` to point the local ref at the new repair commit (line 117) before attempting `pushRepairedV2Generation` (line 134). If the push fails, the function returns `(false, err)` and the caller marks the candidate as \"Failed\", but the local ref has already been advanced. The next migration run will see the new local OID, find the remote unchanged (still at the old OID), and re-attempt with `--force-with-lease=<old-oid>` which now fails because the lease is computed from the *new* local `RefOID` value... wait — `candidate.RefOID` is captured before mutation, so subsequent runs re-list candidates and recompute. The real concern is the local repo is desynced from remote until the next successful push.\n**Impact if not fixed:** After a transient push failure, the user's local ref points at a repair commit that may never reach origin until they re-run migrate (and even then, only if `RepairV2GenerationMetadata` re-detects the staleness — which it does, since `current` will be re-read). So self-healing on retry; the desync window is small. Worth flagging only because the temp-ref path for remote-only candidates avoids this issue cleanly.\n**Evidence:** Order of operations at `generation_repair.go:117` then `134`.\n**Suggested fix:** For `HasRemote=true` candidates with a local OID equal to the remote OID, optionally push first via the source ref's *commit hash* (not the ref) before updating the local ref, or roll back the local ref on push failure. Low priority.\n\n### Finding 3: Test gap — `sortMigratableCheckpoints` zero-time and tie-breaking behavior is not exercised\n\n**Severity:** Medium\n**Category:** test gap\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate_test.go` (no test for `sortMigratableCheckpoints` directly)\n**Issue:** The new sort function has four logical branches (both zero / left zero / right zero / equal / before), but the only tests that exercise it use distinct, non-zero `CreatedAt` values. There is no test that pins down the documented behavior \"zero-time sentinels go to the end\" or \"ties break by checkpoint ID lexicographically\". A future refactor could invert one of these branches without a test failing.\n**Impact if not fixed:** A regression in zero-time placement would silently change packing order for migrated repos that have legacy v1 checkpoints with missing `CreatedAt`. Generation timestamp envelopes would still be correct, but generation membership could shift between archives in confusing ways.\n**Evidence:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:227-244` has 4 distinct branches; only the `default: left.Before(right)` branch is covered by `TestMigrateCheckpointsV2_PacksFullGenerationsOldestFirst`.\n**Suggested fix:** Add a unit test for `sortMigratableCheckpoints` covering: all-zero, mixed zero+non-zero, equal-non-zero (tie on ID), and a normal case.\n\n### Finding 4: Test gap — `RepairV2GenerationMetadata` \"no-op\" paths are unverified\n\n**Severity:** Medium\n**Category:** test gap\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/strategy/generation_repair_test.go`\n**Issue:** Two important no-op paths are untested:\n1. **No archived candidates at all**: `RepairV2GenerationMetadata` should return an empty result with no error. The push-target memoizer should never be called.\n2. **`generation.json` already matches raw transcripts**: `generationMetadataEqual` short-circuit at `generation_repair.go:98-100` should treat the candidate as \"Skipped\", not \"Repaired\" — but no test asserts this. A regression in `generationMetadataEqual` (e.g., comparing `time.Time` without `.Equal`) would silently churn refs.\n**Impact if not fixed:** Idempotency regressions in repair (re-running migrate continually rewriting refs and pushing to origin) would not be caught. The \"no candidates\" case gets exercised indirectly elsewhere but with a clean fast-path return is worth pinning.\n**Evidence:** `generation_repair_test.go` has only two tests, both for the \"stale → repair\" happy path (local + remote-only).\n**Suggested fix:** Add tests for the no-candidates case and the already-correct case. Both can be unit-style against `repairV2GenerationMetadata` directly.\n\n### Finding 5: Test gap — `ComputeGenerationRawTranscriptTimestamps` short-circuit when one checkpoint is missing a timestamp\n\n**Severity:** Low\n**Category:** test gap\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/v2_generation_test.go:507`\n**Issue:** `ComputeGenerationRawTranscriptTimestamps` sets `missingCheckpointTimestamp = true` whenever ANY checkpoint in the tree fails to yield a transcript-derived range (lines 222-232 in `v2_generation.go`), and then returns `(_, false, nil)` so callers fall back to `generation.json`. This all-or-nothing behavior is the key correctness property that lets `cleanup.ListEligibleV2Generations` and `repairOneV2GenerationMetadata` trust the result, but it is not directly tested. The single new test (`TestComputeGenerationRawTranscriptTimestamps_IgnoresMainMetadata`) covers only the happy path with one checkpoint.\n**Impact if not fixed:** A future change that flips `missingCheckpointTimestamp` to a per-checkpoint skip would silently corrupt generation envelopes for partially-readable archives.\n**Evidence:** `cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/v2_generation.go:220-232`; only one test covers this function.\n**Suggested fix:** Add a test that builds a generation tree with two checkpoints — one with a transcript and one without — and asserts `found == false`.\n\n### Finding 6: Quality — leaky `migratedFullCheckpoint{}` zero-value sentinel from `migrateOneCheckpoint`\n\n**Severity:** Low\n**Category:** quality\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:275`, caller at `migrate.go:211`\n**Issue:** `migrateOneCheckpoint` returns three \"shapes\" of `*migratedFullCheckpoint`: `nil` (error path), `&migratedFullCheckpoint{}` (no work to pack), and a populated value (work to pack). The caller checks both `fullCheckpoint != nil` and `len(fullCheckpoint.sessions) > 0`. Two distinct ways to express \"nothing to pack\" is mildly confusing and means a future caller could forget the second check. Returning `nil` for the no-work case would collapse the two sentinels into one.\n**Impact if not fixed:** Cosmetic. No correctness risk today since both call sites correctly OR-check.\n**Evidence:** Lines 211, 275, 287, 377.\n**Suggested fix:** Return `nil` instead of `&migratedFullCheckpoint{}` from the `!queuedFullRepair && backfillErr == nil` branch, and update the caller to a single `if fullCheckpoint != nil` check.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nReviewed 9 changed files against base `4d73c5d6c`. Verified the four \"intentional\" branch-context behaviors specifically called out (sort tie-breaking, `errAlreadyMigrated` semantics through the refactor, fresh-migration `/full/current` creation, `pruneV2ArchivedCheckpointRef` fallback chain) — all check out as documented. No critical findings.\n\nFindings: one inconsistency between `repairOneV2GenerationMetadata` (no fallback) and the migrate-internal `addRecomputedGenerationJSON` (with fallback) on the `found=false` path, one minor robustness concern with local ref mutation before push, and three test gaps around sort behavior, repair no-op paths, and the missing-timestamp short-circuit.\n\nRelevant files: `/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/fix/entire-migrate-checkpoint-order/cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go`, `/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/fix/entire-migrate-checkpoint-order/cmd/entire/cli/strategy/generation_repair.go`, `/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/fix/entire-migrate-checkpoint-order/cmd/entire/cli/strategy/cleanup.go`, `/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/fix/entire-migrate-checkpoint-order/cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/v2_generation.go`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7abcc7481233c42f (use SendMessage with to: 'a7abcc7481233c42f' to continue thi